  <content>
  <section><title>Creating Vertex Form from a Vertex and a Point</title>
<para>Write the quadratic in vertex form that has a vertex at \((3, 4)\) and goes through the point \((1, 2)\).</para>
<para>First start with the vertex form of a quadratic function.</para>
<para>\(y=a(x-h)^2+k\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Substitute the vertex in at \((h, k)\).
  \(y=a(x-3)^2+4\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute the other point at \((x, y)\).
  \(2=a(1-3)^2+4\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Solve for \(a\).
  \(2=a(1-3)^2+4\)
  \(2=a(-2)^2+4\)
  \(2=a(4)+4\)
  \(2-4=a(4)+4-4\)
  \(-2=a(4)\)
  \(-\frac24=\frac{a(4)}{4}\)
  \(a=-\frac12\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Substitute the value of \(a\) value and the vertex \((h, k)\) into the vertex form.
  \(y=-\frac12(x-3)^2+4\)</para>

<note class="try look-at-me"/>
<title>Try It: Creating Vertex Form from a Vertex and a Point</title>

<!--Q#-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Write the quadratic in vertex form that has a vertex at \((2, 5)\) and goes through the point \((3, 7)\).</para>
  
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>Solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(y=2(x-2)^2+5\)</para>
  
  <para>First, start with the vertex form of a quadratic function:
    \(y=a(x-h)^2+k\)</para>
  
  <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Substitute the vertex at \((h, k)\):
    \(y=a(x-2)^2+5\)</para>
  
  <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute the other point at \((x, y)\):
    \(7=a(3-2)^2+5\)</para>
  
  <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Solve for \(a\):
    \(7=a(1)^2+5\)
    \(7-5=a(1)^2+5-5\)
    \(a=2\)</para>
  
  <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Substitute the value of \(a\) and the vertex \((h, k)\) into the vertex form:
    \(y=2(x-2)^2+5\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example><!-- Interaction End -->
</section>
